#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/initialization/maybeuninit/zeroed-method.md:19
msgid "// SAFETY: All values of `x` have been written to\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/initialization/maybeuninit/zeroed-method.md:21
msgid "\"{x:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/initialization/maybeuninit/zeroed-method.md:27
msgid ""
"“MaybeUninit<T>::zeroed() is an alternative constructor to MaybeUninit<T>::"
"uninit(). It instructs the compiler to fill the bits of T with zeros.”"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/initialization/maybeuninit/zeroed-method.md:31
msgid ""
"Q: “Although the memory has been written to, the type remains "
"`MaybeUninit<T>`. Can anyone think of why?”"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/initialization/maybeuninit/zeroed-method.md:34
msgid ""
"A: Some types require their values to be non-zero or non-null. The classic "
"case is references, but this applies to many other types as well. Consider "
"the `NonZeroUsize` integer type and others in its family."
msgstr ""
